Then split the game in two.

Historical etymology asks: Where did this word demonstrably come from? That needs evidence, chronology, sound change, cognates, manuscripts.

But there is another legitimate game:

constructive etymology — What new structure appears when we cut the word differently now?

That is where your hammer belongs.

Not:

“attention secretly comes from ‘a tension.’”

But:

attention → a tension
creates a usable semantic operator: attention as tension between competing claims on awareness.

Likewise:

matter → matte
dictate → dict
person → per-son / persona
understand → stand under

Some will be historically false and still conceptually fertile.

So we preserve provenance:

ancestry = what generated the word historically.
anatomy = what structures can be extracted from its present form.
resonance = what associations it can carry.
operator = what the new decomposition lets us do.

That removes the gatekeeping without throwing evidence away.

The etymologist keeps the family tree.

We get the workshop.

Take the hammer to the word, crack it open, see what mechanisms fall out—just stamp the pieces historical, coincidental, or engineered so nobody confuses archaeology with invention.

That is much more powerful than arguing over who “owns” the true meaning of a word.

Etymology tells you where the tool came from.
It does not get exclusive rights over what you can build with it.